A||N||14||Haze_Performance||The 2002 Pontiac Fiero??  (Kinda Long)||||4||
Z||000000||Haze_Performance||07-29-2000||03:12 PM||||AKA 2002 Pontiac Speedster<P>What's New<BR>   One of the stars of the 1999 Geneva Auto Show, tabbed as most significant by many observers, was the Opel Speedster sports car.  Developed in conjunction with former GM subsidiary Lotus Engineering, the Opel Speedster was based heavily on the existing Lotus Elise and speculation is running rampant that a variation of the design will appear in late 2001 as the sports flagship for Pontiac when the Firebird is dropped.<BR>   There is some talk that the Speedster may not actually be sold in the U.S. But with no sporty cars in the lineup after the Firebird goes away, this could bring the youthful attitude Pontiac is seeking.  The Aztek shows the brands willingness to take a chance and as long as volume ambitiions aren't too high, the Speedster looks much more certain to elevate the brand image Pontiac covets.<P>THE CAR<BR>   The Opel Speedster concept is a lightweight, high performance 2-passanger sports car that combines an aluminum chassis with a composite body.  The chassis owes its origins to the limited production Lotus Elise, a car that holds the reputation as beign one of the best handling production cars available. <BR>   The mid-engine Speedster features Opel's new aluminum, 2.2-liter Ecotec 4-cylinder engine with 150-horsepower.  If the Speedster is produced overseas, it will retain this powerplant.  If the car shows up here, it could have any one of a variety of engines, possibly including one built by Honda.<BR>  To give you an idea of how small the car is, compare it to the Mazda Miata.  At 155.3 inches, the tiny Miata will dwarf the even smaller Speedster by more than half a foot.<BR>   With the expected passing of the Firebird icon in the years to come, Pontiac will sure to be looking for a signature performance vehicle to head its brand.  WIth the development for the Speedster already completed, and a companion vehicle planned for GM brand Vauxhall in England, extending the production to cover a Pontiac derivative makes sense globally.<P>Source: Consumer Review 2001 Car Preview<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Haze_sig_1.gif">  My Website's  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/WS6_Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero</A> &                  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/HMP" TARGET=_blank>H.M.P. </A>||||reg||4
